        details = "Keeps: tax-funded care free at the point of use, from The National Health Service, 1948 (Fabian socialism, Nordic social democracy); one national purchaser covering nearly all people, from Taiwan's national health insurance from 1995 (Developmental state); social insurance for sickness, from Germany's sickness insurance law of 1883 (Social market economy); and a compulsory savings account for health care, from Singapore's MediSave account from 1984 (Monetarist neoliberalism). Blocks: charges added during budget cuts, safeguarded by a parliamentary vote; purchaser favouritism, safeguarded by published prices and an annual independent audit; loss of cover through cuts, safeguarded by a fixed coverage floor; inpatient-heavy care, safeguarded by a ring-fenced primary care budget. Test: coverage share, out-of-pocket share of spending, waiting times and clinic visits, every year from launch. Source: Warwick University Library (secondary); Deutschlandmuseum calendar entry for 1 December 1883; BMC Health Services Research (2010); Dollars and Sense on MediSave (secondary); PubMed Central PMC6220729 (secondary); Johnson (1982) and Harvey (2005), model sources for inferred safeguards.",
    ),
